    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "# Homework 4 {.unnumbered}\n\n\n\n\n\n## Problem 1\n\nIn a certain region, times (minutes) between occurrences of earthquakes (of any magnitude) have a distribution with percentiles displayed in the table below.\n\na.  Construct a spinner corresponding to this distribution.\nb.  What percent of times are between 26.8 and 110.0 minutes?\nc.  Let $F$ be the cdf. Evaluate and interpret $F(42.8)$.\nd.  Let $Q$ be the quantile function. Evaluate and interpret $Q(0.9)$.\ne.  Sketch (by hand) a histogram of this distribution.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\\begin{table}\n\\centering\n\\begin{tabular}[t]{l|r}\n\\hline\nPercentile & Value (minutes)\\\\\n\\hline\n10th & 12.6\\\\\n\\hline\n20th & 26.8\\\\\n\\hline\n30th & 42.8\\\\\n\\hline\n40th & 61.3\\\\\n\\hline\n50th & 83.2\\\\\n\\hline\n60th & 110.0\\\\\n\\hline\n70th & 144.5\\\\\n\\hline\n80th & 193.1\\\\\n\\hline\n90th & 276.3\\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Problem 2\n\n(Continued from previous HW.) In the Regina/Cady problem, let $W=|R-Y|$ be the amount of time the first person to arrive has to wait for the second person.\nRecall that $W$ is a continuous random variable with pdf $$\nf_W(w) = 2(1-w), \\quad 0 < w < 1.\n$$ a.\nLet $F$ be the cdf.\nEvaluate and interpret $F(0.25)$.\na.\nFind an expression for the cdf of $W$.\nSet up an integral, but sketch a picture and use geometry to compute.\nb.\nFind and interpret the 25th percentile of $W$.\n(You can do the next part first if you want, but it might help to start with a specific number like in this part.) c.\nFind the quantile function of $W$.\nd.\nSketch a spinner corresponding to the distribution of $W$.\nLabel the 25th, 50th, and 75th percentiles.\ne.\n**Coding required.** Use your simulation results from before to approximate the 25th, 50th, and 75th percentiles.\n\n## Problem 3\n\nSolve Example 11.3 from the Normal distribution handout (the problem on daily high temperatures in SLO.)\n\n## Problem 4\n\nSolve Example 11.4 from the Normal distribution handout (the problem on \"effect size\".)\n\n## Problem 5\n\nThe latest series of collectible Lego Minifigures contains 3 different Minifigure prizes (labeled 1, 2, 3).\nEach package contains a single unknown prize.\nSuppose we only buy 3 packages and we consider as our sample space outcome the results of just these 3 packages (prize in package 1, prize in package 2, prize in package 3).\nFor example, 323 (or (3, 2, 3)) represents prize 3 in the first package, prize 2 in the second package, prize 3 in the third package.\nLet $X$ be the number of distinct prizes obtained in these 3 packages.\nLet $Y$ be the number of these 3 packages that contain prize 1.\nSuppose that each package is equally likely to contain any of the 3 prizes, regardless of the contents of other packages.\nThere are 27 possible, equally likely outcomes\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\\begin{table}\n\\centering\n\\begin{tabular}[t]{r|r|r|l|l}\n\\hline\nbox1 & box2 & box3 & X & Y\\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 1 & 1 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 1 & 1 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 1 & 1 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 2 & 1 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 2 & 1 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 2 & 1 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 3 & 1 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 3 & 1 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 3 & 1 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 1 & 2 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 1 & 2 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 1 & 2 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 2 & 2 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 2 & 2 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 2 & 2 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 3 & 2 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 3 & 2 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 3 & 2 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 1 & 3 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 1 & 3 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 1 & 3 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 2 & 3 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 2 & 3 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 2 & 3 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n1 & 3 & 3 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 3 & 3 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n3 & 3 & 3 &  & \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\na.  Evaluate $X$ and $Y$ for each of the outcomes.\nb.  Construct a two-way table representing the *joint distribution* of $X$ and $Y$.\nc.  Sketch a plot representing the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$.\nd.  Construct a spinner corresponding to the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$.\ne.  Describe two ways to simulate an $(X, Y)$ pair.\nf.  Identify the *marginal* distribution of $X$, and construct a corresponding spinner.\ng.  Identify the *marginal* distribution of $Y$, and construct a corresponding spinner.\nh.  Describe in detail in words how you could conduct a simulation and use the results to approximate. (This part is asking you to describe the process in words; not to write code.)\n    i. $\\text{P}(X = 3)$\n    i. $\\text{P}(X = 2, Y = 1)$\n    i. $\\text{E}(X)$\n    i. $\\text{E}(Y)$\n    i. $\\text{E}(XY)$\n    i. $\\text{SD}(X)$\n    i. $\\text{SD}(Y)$\n    1. $\\text{Cov}(X, Y)$\n    i. $\\text{Corr}(X, Y)$\ni.  **Coding required.** Code and run the simulation from the previous part and use the simulation results to approximate each of\n    i. $\\text{P}(X = 3)$\n    i. $\\text{P}(X = 2, Y = 1)$\n    i. $\\text{E}(X)$\n    i. $\\text{E}(Y)$\n    i. $\\text{E}(XY)$\n    i. $\\text{SD}(X)$\n    i. $\\text{SD}(Y)$\n    1. $\\text{Cov}(X, Y)$\n    i. $\\text{Corr}(X, Y)$\nj.  Compute\n    i. $\\text{P}(X = 3)$\n    i. $\\text{P}(X = 2, Y = 1)$\n    i. $\\text{E}(X)$ iii.$\\text{E}(Y)$\n    i. $\\text{E}(XY)$\n    i. $\\text{SD}(X)$ iii.$\\text{SD}(Y)$\n    1. $\\text{Cov}(X, Y)$\n    i. $\\text{Corr}(X, Y)$\n\n",
